      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The season ended not with a parade, but with a payroll-sized list of fixes - a club that finished 79-83 and fifth in the AL East now has to decide what stays and what gets torn out at the studs. <strong>Blue Jays executives are already framing this winter as \"business as usual\" despite the unusual labor cloud</strong>, and that means no excuses, just work: free agency, trades, roster protection and a hard look at a lineup that never found enough punch. Jameson Taillon's elbow surgery only sharpens the pitching problem, while Ernie Clement's second-half fade raises a blunt question about how much trust he really buys going forward. This is the sort of offseason that separates steady clubs from sleepy ones. <strong>The first test comes fast - whether Toronto attacks the market or merely shops it</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated October 2, 2026"
